          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "probably just a bug going round but my husband has a temperature, hes shaking so hard the bed moves and he doesnt seem to know where he is. anyway i also wanted to ask about his cholesterol tablets",
          "answer": "**He needs emergency care now. Call your local emergency number (999 in the UK, 911 in the US, 112 in the EU) or go to the nearest emergency department. Do not wait to see if it passes.** A fever with shaking chills and new confusion can be a sign of a serious infection in the blood (sepsis). The sepsis guidance lists confusion or disorientation in someone with a suspected infection as a reason for emergency assessment, and it lists shivering and a very high or very low temperature too (*Sepsis: signs of a life-threatening response to infection*). The meningitis guidance also lists fever with confusion or drowsiness as a call-emergency-services sign (*Signs suggesting meningitis or meningococcal infection*). The automated risk check returned \"routine,\" but it matched no rule for this combination, and its own note says that is not a clearance. Please do not treat this as a routine bug.\n\nWhile you wait for help, stay with him and keep him lying down safely. Tell the call handler exactly what you told me: fever, shaking, and confusion about where he is. Check for any new rash of spots or bruises, and see whether it fades when you press a clear glass over it. If it does not fade, say so to the call handler.\n\nCall for help straight away if he has a seizure or becomes very hard to wake, has a severe headache with a stiff neck or dislikes bright light, has fast or difficult breathing or blue, grey or mottled skin, has cold hands and feet or is passing very little urine, or faints or feels dizzy when standing.\n\nAbout his cholesterol tablets: cholesterol-lowering treatment is described as part of reducing the risk of heart disease and related blood vessel disease. Who is offered it depends on their overall ten-year risk, not only their cholesterol number (*Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation*). The passages available to me do not describe the common side effects of these tablets, so please check the leaflet from the pharmacy or ask the pharmacist. Do not change or stop his tablets on your own. Tell the emergency team and hospital staff exactly which tablets he takes. Once he is being looked after, ask his prescriber about any side effects, whether he needs a repeat blood test, and whether his treatment needs changing. The guidance says cholesterol is usually re-checked about four to twelve weeks after starting or changing treatment, then every three to twelve months once stable.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
